um\">78.17 ms</td><td class=\"num\">1</td><td class=\"num\">0.8%</td><td class=\"mono\">c77c0b68bc83581fd4fcb7f9</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n<p class=\"muted\">Environment: status=SUCCEEDED, approved_by=setup, backend=docker, image=perfpilot-sandbox:latest, image_digest=sha256:344f19264de844b5bf76b33f9aecb93846a9e0ef0ec5bdd13ea55a520cc545b8, python=3.12.15, platform=Linux-7.0.14-linuxkit-aarch64-with-glibc2.41, network=none, workdir=/workspace, dependencies=image only, cpus=2.0, memory_mb=2048, pids=256, timeout_seconds=600, exit_code=0, duration_s (incl. container start/stop)=3.860122</p><p class=\"muted\">Command run once in the isolated sandbox (network disabled) under cProfile + tracemalloc (concurrent). Times include profiler overhead; compare functions by their share of profiled time. The table lists repository functions ordered by attributed time (self time plus direct library calls); &#39;Share&#39; is the exclusive-time share.</p><p class=\"muted\">Peak RSS (resident memory): getrusage ru_maxrss of the profiled process inside the sandbox. It includes the interpreter, imported modules and the profiling harness.</p><p class=\"muted\">Peak traced memory (tracemalloc): peak of allocations made through Python&#39;s allocator during the command; memory allocated outside it (e.g. by C extensions) is not counted.</p><p class=\"muted\">18 static finding(s) matched measured functions; their priority was re-ranked with measured_factor = 1 + 2 x attributed share (see section 5).</p><p class=\"muted\">Runtime-only hotspots (hot, but no static finding): run (benchmarks/run_workload.py:23) 21.1%; build_catalog (shopfast/catalog.py:21) 8.4%; price_with_tax (shopfast/pricing.py:30) 1.7%.</p></div>\n</section>\n\n<section>\n<h2 id=\"s8\"><span class=\"secnum\">8</span> Algorithmic complexity analysis</h2>\n<p class=\"muted\">Complexity figures are estimates derived from code structure (loop nesting, collection operations). They are not measurements and assume the input sizes implied by the code.</p>\n<table class=\"data\">\n  <thead><tr><th width=\"5%\">#</th><th width=\"47%\">Finding</th><th width=\"33%\">Estimate</th><th width=\"15%\">Provenance</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>1</td><td>Nested loops matching items by equality<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/inventory_sync.py:11-12</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: O(n\u00b7m) comparisons for n outer and m inner items; a dict index built once makes it O(n + m).</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>2</td><td>Database query inside a loop (N+1)<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/orders.py:13-15</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: N loop iterations issue N queries; a batched query needs 1-2 round-trips.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>3</td><td>Database query inside a loop (N+1)<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/orders.py:19-21</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: N loop iterations issue N queries; a batched query needs 1-2 round-trips.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>4</td><td>Re-sorting a growing collection inside a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/catalog.py:66</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: O(n\u00b2 log n) total for n iterations vs O(n log n) once.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>6</td><td>List membership test inside a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/catalog.py:49</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: O(n\u00b7m) comparisons for n loop iterations over a list of m items; a set/dict makes each test O(1) on average.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>8</td><td>List used as a queue (pop(0)/insert(0, \u2026)) in a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/inventory_sync.py:32</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: O(n) per front operation, O(n\u00b2) over n iterations; deque makes it O(n).</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>9</td><td>List membership test inside a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/inventory_sync.py:33</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: O(n\u00b7m) comparisons for n loop iterations over a list of m items; a set/dict makes each test O(1) on average.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>10</td><td>List membership test inside a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/inventory_sync.py:42</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: O(n\u00b7m) comparisons for n loop iterations over a list of m items; a set/dict makes each test O(1) on average.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>11</td><td>List membership test inside a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/catalog.py:41</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: O(n\u00b7m) comparisons for n loop iterations over a list of m items; a set/dict makes each test O(1) on average.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>12</td><td>String built by repeated concatenation in a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/reporting.py:17</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: up to O(n\u00b2) characters copied for n appended pieces when the string is not resized in place; &#39;&#39;.join is O(total length).</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>13</td><td>Linear list search/removal inside a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/inventory_sync.py:42</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: O(n\u00b7m) for n iterations over a list of m items; a dict/Counter/set lookup is O(1) on average.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>14</td><td>Loop-invariant computation repeated every iteration<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/pricing.py:42</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: the call&#39;s cost is paid once per iteration instead of once in total.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>15</td><td>Sequential HTTP request inside a loop<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/api.py:47</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: N iterations \u2192 N sequential network round-trips.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>17</td><td>Overlapping recursion without memoization<br><span class=\"mono loc\">shopfast/utils.py:8</span></td><td><span class=\"estimate\">estimate</span> estimate: without memoization the call count can grow exponentially with the input (e.g. O(2^n)); with it, proportional to the number of distinct arguments.</td><td><span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span></td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</section>\n\n<section>\n<h2 id=\"s9\"><span class=\"secnum\">9</span> Proposed optimization strategies</h2>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"card-title\">1. <span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span> <span class=\"badge effort\">MEDIUM</span> Stop blocking the event loop in async code in 2 functions of shopfast/api.py</p>\n  <p>2 blocking call inside an async function locations in 2 functions in shopfast/api.py. `time.sleep(RATE_LIMIT_SECONDS)` sleeps the whole thread inside `async def get_product`.</p>\n  <table class=\"kv\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><td>Use async equivalents or offload the blocking call with `asyncio.to_thread`; for synchronous database sessions, choose one async/sync boundary per endpoint.\n- Use the async equivalent (`await asyncio.sleep`, `httpx.AsyncClient`/`aiohttp`, `asyncio.create_subprocess_exec`, an async DB driver or `AsyncSession`, `aiofiles`), or move the call off the event loop with `await asyncio.to_thread(func, ...)`.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Rationale</th><td>Static evidence (not measured): `time.sleep(RATE_LIMIT_SECONDS)` sleeps the whole thread inside `async def get_product`. `urllib.request.urlopen(EXCHANGE_URL.format(currency=currenc\u2026` performs a blocking HTTP request inside `async def fetch_exchange_rate`. The top location is a request handler.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Expected effect (hypothesis)</th><td>Other requests and timers keep making progress while the slow operation runs; the effect shows up as better tail latency under concurrent load.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Correctness risks</th><td>Offloaded code must be thread-safe; async libraries differ in timeouts, exceptions and session lifecycles. Thread offloading adds thread-pool overhead and requires the code to be thread-safe; async libraries have different APIs, timeouts and exceptions; very short blocking calls (small local files) may not be worth changing.</td></tr>    <tr><th>Uncertainty</th><td>Derived from 2 static finding(s) (confidence: HIGH x2). Static analysis cannot see input sizes, call frequency or the database/network latency, so the real impact is unknown until the code is profiled before and after the change.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Findings</th><td class=\"mono\">3a6884ee87bb58683f088133, a9ad1976044aecb02a4f50f9</td></tr><tr><th>Citations</th><td class=\"mono\">shopfast/api.py:19, shopfast/api.py:31</td></tr>    <tr><th>Generated by</th><td>deterministic</td></tr>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"card-title\">2. <span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span> <span class=\"badge effort\">SMALL</span> Use a set for repeated membership tests in unique_categories (shopfast/catalog.py)</p>\n  <p>1 list membership test inside a loop location in `unique_categories` (shopfast/catalog.py). `product.category not in categories` runs inside a loop and scans `categories`, a list that also grows inside the loop.</p>\n  <table class=\"kv\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><td>Replace repeated linear scans of a list with hash lookups.\n- Build a set (or dict) once before the loop, e.g. `allowed = set(items)`, and test `x in allowed`; when the list grows inside the loop, maintain a parallel set.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Rationale</th><td>Static evidence (not measured): `product.category not in categories` runs inside a loop and scans `categories`, a list that also grows inside the loop. Complexity estimate: O(n\u00b7m) comparisons for n loop iterations over a list of m items; a set/dict makes each test O(1) on average. The top location is library code imported by request-handling modules. Execution path: shopfast.api -&gt; shopfast.catalog:unique_categories.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Expected effect (hypothesis)</th><td>Each membership test no longer scans the whole list, so the loop scales with the number of iterations instead of iterations times list length; negligible for short lists.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Correctness risks</th><td>Elements must be hashable, and a set does not preserve order or duplicates. Set elements must be hashable; a set loses ordering and duplicates (keep the list if you need them); building it costs O(m) time and extra memory, so it only pays off when the test runs many times.</td></tr>    <tr><th>Uncertainty</th><td>Derived from 1 static finding(s) (confidence: HIGH x1). Static analysis cannot see input sizes, call frequency or the database/network latency, so the real impact is unknown until the code is profiled before and after the change.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Findings</th><td class=\"mono\">edab9a09628ae121c2232ad6</td></tr><tr><th>Citations</th><td class=\"mono\">shopfast/catalog.py:49</td></tr>    <tr><th>Generated by</th><td>deterministic</td></tr>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"card-title\">3. <span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span> <span class=\"badge effort\">SMALL</span> Avoid re-sorting a growing collection in top_rated (shopfast/catalog.py)</p>\n  <p>1 re-sorting a growing collection inside a loop location in `top_rated` (shopfast/catalog.py). `best` grows inside the loop at line 64 and is sorted again on every iteration.</p>\n  <table class=\"kv\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><td>Sort once after the loop, or keep the collection ordered incrementally.\n- Sort once after the loop, or keep the collection ordered incrementally with `bisect.insort` / a `heapq` priority queue when the order is needed during the loop.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Rationale</th><td>Static evidence (not measured): `best` grows inside the loop at line 64 and is sorted again on every iteration. Complexity estimate: O(n\u00b2 log n) total for n iterations vs O(n log n) once. The top location is library code imported by request-handling modules. Execution path: shopfast.api -&gt; shopfast.catalog:top_rated.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Expected effect (hypothesis)</th><td>Avoids sorting the whole collection on every iteration.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Correctness risks</th><td>Sort stability and key functions must stay identical. `heapq` only guarantees the smallest item is first (not a fully sorted list); `bisect.insort` is O(n) per insert because of list shifting but avoids repeated O(n log n) sorts; sort stability and key functions must be preserved.</td></tr>    <tr><th>Uncertainty</th><td>Derived from 1 static finding(s) (confidence: HIGH x1). Static analysis cannot see input sizes, call frequency or the database/network latency, so the real impact is unknown until the code is profiled before and after the change.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Findings</th><td class=\"mono\">2c998d23032475003d99732b</td></tr><tr><th>Citations</th><td class=\"mono\">shopfast/catalog.py:66</td></tr>    <tr><th>Generated by</th><td>deterministic</td></tr>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"card-title\">4. <span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span> <span class=\"badge effort\">MEDIUM</span> Batch database queries issued inside loops (N+1) in order_summaries (shopfast/orders.py)</p>\n  <p>2 database query inside a loop (n+1) locations in `order_summaries` (shopfast/orders.py). DB-API `execute()` (`cursor.execute(&#39;SELECT id, customer_id, total_cents FROM or\u2026`) runs once per iteration of the loop at line 12.</p>\n  <table class=\"kv\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><td>Fetch everything the loop needs with one query (IN-list, eager loading, or a join) before the loop, index the rows in a dict, and batch writes.\n- Load what the loop needs with one query before it (`WHERE id IN (...)`, `select(...).where(Model.id.in_(ids))`, `selectinload`/`joinedload`, Django `select_related`/`prefetch_related`/`in_bulk`) and look rows up in a dict; batch writes with `executemany`, bulk inserts, or a single UPDATE.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Rationale</th><td>Static evidence (not measured): DB-API `execute()` (`cursor.execute(&#39;SELECT id, customer_id, total_cents FROM or\u2026`) runs once per iteration of the loop at line 12. DB-API `execute()` (`conn.execute(&#39;SELECT name, tier FROM customers WHERE id = ?\u2026`) runs once per iteration of the loop at line 12. Complexity estimate: N loop iterations issue N queries; a batched query needs 1-2 round-trips. The top location is a CLI command or script. Execution path: shopfast.app:main -&gt; shopfast.orders:order_summaries.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Expected effect (hypothesis)</th><td>Replaces one database round trip per item with a constant number of round trips; the benefit grows with the number of items and with database latency. This code is a CLI command or script, so the practical benefit is probably limited to that workflow.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Correctness risks</th><td>Large IN-lists may need chunking; eager loading can fetch more columns than needed; transaction boundaries and per-row error handling may change. One large query can return more data and use more memory; IN-lists have database limits (chunk them); batching writes changes transaction and error-reporting granularity; eager loading can over-fetch when only a few rows are used.</td></tr>    <tr><th>Uncertainty</th><td>Derived from 2 static finding(s) (confidence: HIGH x2). Static analysis cannot see input sizes, call frequency or the database/network latency, so the real impact is unknown until the code is profiled before and after the change.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Findings</th><td class=\"mono\">cf7602f9ab833fb62bbed6cd, 1da354509e0a36d96a4b7c34</td></tr><tr><th>Citations</th><td class=\"mono\">shopfast/orders.py:13-15, shopfast/orders.py:19-21</td></tr>    <tr><th>Generated by</th><td>deterministic</td></tr>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"card-title\">5. <span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span> <span class=\"badge effort\">MEDIUM</span> Replace a nested-loop join with a dict index in reconcile (shopfast/inventory_sync.py)</p>\n  <p>1 nested loops matching items by equality location in `reconcile` (shopfast/inventory_sync.py). For every item of `warehouse_rows` (line 10) this code scans all of `catalog` looking for `row[&#39;sku&#39;] == product.sku`.</p>\n  <table class=\"kv\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><td>Index the inner collection by the join key once, then look items up.\n- Index the inner collection once by the join key, e.g. `by_key = {b.key: b for b in B}` (a dict of lists for non-unique keys), then look up `by_key.get(a.key)` in a single loop.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Rationale</th><td>Static evidence (not measured): For every item of `warehouse_rows` (line 10) this code scans all of `catalog` looking for `row[&#39;sku&#39;] == product.sku`. Complexity estimate: O(n\u00b7m) comparisons for n outer and m inner items; a dict index built once makes it O(n + m). The top location is internal code with no known path from an entry point.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Expected effect (hypothesis)</th><td>Turns a quadratic pairwise comparison into a single pass plus lookups; the benefit grows with both collection sizes.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Correctness risks</th><td>Keys must be hashable; non-unique keys need a dict of lists, and first-match order must be preserved if the original loop stopped at the first match. Join keys must be hashable; duplicate keys need a dict of lists to keep every match; the index costs O(m) memory; result order can differ from the nested-loop order unless you preserve it explicitly.</td></tr>    <tr><th>Uncertainty</th><td>Derived from 1 static finding(s) (confidence: HIGH x1). Static analysis cannot see input sizes, call frequency or the database/network latency, so the real impact is unknown until the code is profiled before and after the change.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Findings</th><td class=\"mono\">641513bfe8cfaea119ecde22</td></tr><tr><th>Citations</th><td class=\"mono\">shopfast/inventory_sync.py:11-12</td></tr>    <tr><th>Generated by</th><td>deterministic</td></tr>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"card-title\">6. <span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span> <span class=\"badge effort\">SMALL</span> Use a set for repeated membership tests in filter_discontinued (shopfast/catalog.py)</p>\n  <p>1 list membership test inside a loop location in `filter_discontinued` (shopfast/catalog.py). `product.sku not in discontinued_skus` runs inside a loop and scans `discontinued_skus`, which is a list.</p>\n  <table class=\"kv\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><td>Replace repeated linear scans of a list with hash lookups.\n- Build a set (or dict) once before the loop, e.g. `allowed = set(items)`, and test `x in allowed`; when the list grows inside the loop, maintain a parallel set.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Rationale</th><td>Static evidence (not measured): `product.sku not in discontinued_skus` runs inside a loop and scans `discontinued_skus`, which is a list. Complexity estimate: O(n\u00b7m) comparisons for n loop iterations over a list of m items; a set/dict makes each test O(1) on average. The top location is library code imported by request-handling modules. Execution path: shopfast.api -&gt; shopfast.catalog:filter_discontinued.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Expected effect (hypothesis)</th><td>Each membership test no longer scans the whole list, so the loop scales with the number of iterations instead of iterations times list length; negligible for short lists.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Correctness risks</th><td>Elements must be hashable, and a set does not preserve order or duplicates. Set elements must be hashable; a set loses ordering and duplicates (keep the list if you need them); building it costs O(m) time and extra memory, so it only pays off when the test runs many times.</td></tr>    <tr><th>Uncertainty</th><td>Derived from 1 static finding(s) (confidence: MEDIUM x1). Static analysis cannot see input sizes, call frequency or the database/network latency, so the real impact is unknown until the code is profiled before and after the change.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Findings</th><td class=\"mono\">7b2edb85bdd1bacc3b7894b1</td></tr><tr><th>Citations</th><td class=\"mono\">shopfast/catalog.py:41</td></tr>    <tr><th>Generated by</th><td>deterministic</td></tr>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"card-title\">7. <span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span> <span class=\"badge effort\">MEDIUM</span> Avoid sequential HTTP requests in a loop in fetch_rates (shopfast/api.py)</p>\n  <p>1 sequential http request inside a loop location in `fetch_rates` (shopfast/api.py). `urllib.request.urlopen(EXCHANGE_URL.format(currency=currenc\u2026` issues an HTTP request on every iteration of the loop at line 46, one after another.</p>\n  <table class=\"kv\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><td>Use a batch endpoint, or issue requests concurrently with a bound and one client.\n- Use a batch endpoint if the API has one; otherwise issue the requests concurrently with a bound (`asyncio.gather` + `asyncio.Semaphore`, or a `ThreadPoolExecutor` for sync clients), reuse one client/session, and cache repeated responses.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Rationale</th><td>Static evidence (not measured): `urllib.request.urlopen(EXCHANGE_URL.format(currency=currenc\u2026` issues an HTTP request on every iteration of the loop at line 46, one after another. Complexity estimate: N iterations \u2192 N sequential network round-trips. The top location is library code imported by request-handling modules.</td></tr>\n    <tr><th>Expected effect (hypothesis)</th><td>Network waits overlap instead of adding up one after another.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Correctness risks</th><td>Respect remote rate limits; ordering and partial-failure handling change. Concurrency increases load on the remote service and can trigger rate limits; error handling, retries and result ordering get more complex; cached responses can be stale.</td></tr>    <tr><th>Uncertainty</th><td>Derived from 1 static finding(s) (confidence: HIGH x1). Static analysis cannot see input sizes, call frequency or the database/network latency, so the real impact is unknown until the code is profiled before and after the change.</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Findings</th><td class=\"mono\">56f7ec8c437357e097e16a90</td></tr><tr><th>Citations</th><td class=\"mono\">shopfast/api.py:47</td></tr>    <tr><th>Generated by</th><td>deterministic</td></tr>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"card-title\">8. <span class=\"badge prov prov-static\">STATIC</span> <span class=\"badge effort\">SMALL</span> Use collections.deque for queue operations in restock_order (shopfast/inventory_sync.py)</p>\n  <p>1 list used as a queue (pop(0)/insert(0, \u2026)) in a loop location in `restock_order` (shopfast/inventory_sync.py). `queue.pop(0)` removes/inserts at the front of a list inside a loop.</p>\n  <table class=\"kv\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><td>Use a deque so removals and insertions at the front do not shift the list.\n- Use `collections.deque` and `popleft()` / `appendleft()`, which are O(1) at both ends.